# ── Chapter 10 fidelity pins: "Contribution" ─────────────────────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/10-contribution.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# The chapter's central claim — that recognition has no quantity, and is never
# ranked — is a claim about an ABSENCE and so cannot be pinned by a query. It is
# checked structurally instead, by the guards in verify.sh section 4 — listed here
# without a count, because this list has already grown once since it was written:
#   - no digit in an enacted line          (a quantity cannot be written)
#   - nothing reads `reward`               (recognition feeds nothing downstream)
#   - no rule joins `teaches` or `work` with itself
#                                          (a quantity cannot be counted either)
# The last two are the "What is lost" section: degree is available here and is
# refused, not merely unbuilt. Do not weaken them without rewriting :112-138.
#
# A FOURTH GUARD, verify.sh section 4a: `reward` is arity 1 by decision. Giving it
# a second place to record what the recognition was minted FOR — provenance — is
# refused (2026-08-01, reasoning in CLAUDE.md). It defends :19-21 and :105
# directly: arity 2 makes recognition a fact about a person AND a source, which is
# no longer "the same single fact".
#
# WHY IT IS IN verify.sh AND NOT LEFT TO THESE PINS. The three TRUE pins below do
# catch a rewrite of the heads — measured, `reward(Esa/Quin/Gia)` flip FALSE. But
# the three FALSE pins cannot catch anything: under arity 2 `reward/1` stops
# existing, so they keep answering FALSE and keep passing, against a relation that
# is no longer there. Two more pins elsewhere go vacuous the same way,
# `reward(Cira)` in 06-clawback and `reward(Dev)` in rights-floor. And the ADDITIVE
# shape is invisible to every pin in the suite: a fourth head at arity 2 beside the
# three leaves `reward/1` deriving and everything green. Never let this file drift
# to FALSE-only pins on `reward`.

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closed the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact now aborts the run instead of loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

:expect-pins 27

# "Esa taught Fin, and is recognised."
? teaches(Esa, Fin).
# => TRUE

? reward(Esa).
# => TRUE

# "Quin did the census, and is recognised."
? work(Quin, Census).
# => TRUE

? reward(Quin).
# => TRUE

# "Gia examined Bela's conduct without deceit, and is recognised."
? judge(Gia, Bela).
# => TRUE

? capture(Gia, Bela).
# => TRUE

? reward(Gia).
# => TRUE

# "Bela taught Cira — a real act ... Bela is not recognised, because Bela was
#  voided. The teaching happened and produces nothing."
? teaches(Bela, Cira).
# => TRUE

? false(Bela).
# => TRUE

? reward(Bela).
# => FALSE

# "Lupo examined Mira and lied about it, and earns nothing"
? reward(Lupo).
# => FALSE

# "Dev judged his own child ... earns nothing either"
? reward(Dev).
# => FALSE

# ── THE THIRD DOOR IS GATED ON VOIDING, and it was not always ────────────────
# Vex is chapter 5's carried-void auditor: voided last epoch, still seated on
# paper, the credential refused — and a real judge + capture pair against Tyr
# on the record. Until v0.9 the examiner rule read ~deceive & ~broken only,
# and reward(Vex) answered TRUE: recognition minting for examinations the
# voiding made incapable of counting toward anything. The absence of this pin
# is what let "All three doors close for the same reason" reach print false.
# Negative-controlled against the pre-guard constitution: this reward pin and
# the closing Bela pin flip TRUE, and nothing else in the file moves.
? false(Vex).
# => TRUE

? reward(Vex).
# => FALSE

# ── CARE, ONE ROUTE EACH. This block and everything below it write ground ────
# facts, which persist; the fixture tail stays after every ordinary query.
# "it walks through two of the three." Two fresh people, one door apiece, so
# each route is isolated by construction. Nima is the teaching half: a parent
# who teaches, with the relationship pinned on the record beside the reward —
# the same pair that voids a JUDGE (Article 5, Dev and Esa) disqualifies no
# teacher. Sata is the tending half: one work entry, nothing else, and the
# task constant is free vocabulary like any other.
person(Nima).
parent(Nima, Pico).
teaches(Nima, Pico).

? parent(Nima, Pico).
# => TRUE

? reward(Nima).
# => TRUE

person(Sata).
work(Sata, Care).

? reward(Sata).
# => TRUE

# ...and being tended, like being taught, mints nothing for the recipient —
# the grades ruling's shape, re-exhibited on the care side.
? reward(Pico).
# => FALSE

# ── THE DOORS DO NOT ASK HOW OLD YOU ARE ─────────────────────────────────────
# Cira is the design's own child — person without mature, the whole of the vote
# chapter's example — and the grades ruling refused to let the record hold a
# mark for what she was taught. Record a contribution instead and she is
# recognised, which is the same principle read from the other side. The work
# door is isolated here by construction: Cira has no other minting route.
? prevents(Cira, GeneralAdulthoodDenial).
# => FALSE

work(Cira, Chores).

? reward(Cira).
# => TRUE

# ...and the teaching door, isolated by file order: the care block above pins
# reward(Pico) FALSE while he is only tended and taught. Here Pico teaches, and
# the same query flips. The record's answer changed because Pico contributed —
# the design in two adjacent verdicts.
teaches(Pico, Ona).

? reward(Pico).
# => TRUE

# ── THE VOID CLOSES THE DOOR FORWARD. Ground facts persist; keep this last ───
# Bela — voided, the chapter's teaching-door exhibit — now examines somebody.
# The record holds a real judgment and a real documented finding, the acts the
# examiner rule pays for, and the void stops the mint: "it closes the routes
# by which you would rebuild." Before v0.9 this pair minted — reward(Bela)
# TRUE beside false(Bela) TRUE and lose(Points, Bela) TRUE, the clawback
# recording a loss while the third door was still handing recognition out.
judge(Bela, Ivo).
capture(Bela, Ivo).
# [v0.9] The ground is recorded too, so the void below is the ONLY failing
# conjunct — without this line the FALSE would also hold for want of a cite,
# and the exhibit would stop isolating what it exists to isolate.
cite(Bela, Query, Ivo).

? reward(Bela).
# => FALSE

? lose(Points, Bela).
# => TRUE

# ── [v0.9 2026-08-02] THE EMPTY PAIR STOPS PAYING ────────────────────────────
# The grounds ruling, exercised. Yano — fresh, clean, in good standing — writes
# the pair that used to mint on its own: a judgment and a documented finding,
# no pen, no grounds. Nothing arrives. The third entry names the why — any
# word fills the slot, which is the guard's stated limit — and the pay
# arrives with the author of the reason on record.
judge(Yano, Adam).
capture(Yano, Adam).

? reward(Yano).
# => FALSE

cite(Yano, Hunch, Adam).

? reward(Yano).
# => TRUE

# ── The fourth condition: the door does not pay the recalled ─────────────────
# Boss holds a real recorded examination of Rebel (judge + capture, both in the
# constitution's cast) and is recalled. The reward rule reads ~broken directly,
# and that guard is not the voiding guard beside it: a recall is not a voiding,
# so ~false would never catch this. Baseline FALSE first, then the grounds are
# supplied — the one condition the Yano block above proved was missing — and the
# door still refuses. What is doing the refusing is the recall mark by itself.
? reward(Boss).
# => FALSE

cite(Boss, Findings, Rebel).

? reward(Boss).
# => FALSE
